Despite the availability of rich multimodal data, traditional rule-based RAN management systems rely heavily on domain experts to handcraft rules and select meaningful features for each task. More recent learning-based approaches [8, 15, 30, 31] shift from rule engineering to model engineering, training a task-specific model for each task; while effective within their designated tasks, they require redesign and retraining for new tasks, limiting reuse across diverse downstream tasks. These limitations motivate a single pretrained model that learns reusable representations of RAN behavior and can be efficiently adapted to a wide range of downstream tasks. Recent work such as NetLLM [38] explores pretrained LLMs for networking. LLMs provide rich semantic and contextual priors for tabular and time-series data [7, 17, 40], but their language-oriented representations are less aligned with continuous values and do not reliably capture the fine-grained temporal dependencies required for numerical forecasting [35, 41]. These complementary strengths motivate us to combine LLM-derived representations with a RAN-specific model pretrained directly on operational data.

